1
00:00:06,333 --> 00:00:10,500
Take a matrix W. It turns the unit circle into an ellipse.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:16,733
It stretches one direction by σ1 and another by σ2: those are its singular values.

3
00:00:16,733 --> 00:00:22,866
If every stretch were exactly 1, the circle would stay a circle, and W would be orthogonal.

4
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:29,100
Optimizers like Muon want this for every weight update: same directions, every stretch 1.

5
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:36,766
An SVD could do it, but it is slow on a GPU. The Newton–Schulz iteration uses only matrix products.

6
00:00:36,766 --> 00:00:43,766
Let's apply it a few times and watch the ellipse.

7
00:00:43,766 --> 00:00:50,966
After a handful of steps, the ellipse is a circle. Why does this work? And can it fail?

8
00:00:50,966 --> 00:00:58,166
To see why, write W with its SVD. U and V only rotate; all the stretching sits in Σ.

9
00:00:58,166 --> 00:01:05,566
Plug it into the iteration. U and V pass through untouched; only the middle Σ changes.

10
00:01:05,566 --> 00:01:13,433
Σ is diagonal, so each singular value is updated on its own: σ becomes p(σ).

11
00:01:13,433 --> 00:01:18,866
And p is just this cubic. So the whole matrix question is about a single number.

12
00:01:18,866 --> 00:01:25,966
Start at some positive σ, apply p over and over. Where does it end up? That is part (e).

13
00:01:25,966 --> 00:01:36,066
No theory yet. Let's just try numbers. Start at 0.5: 0.69, 0.87, 0.98, then 1.

14
00:01:36,066 --> 00:01:41,133
1.3 drops to 0.85, then climbs back up to 1.

15
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:46,666
Even a tiny 0.1 creeps up, slowly at first, and also reaches 1.

16
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:53,733
Everything lands on 1. That's no accident: p(1) = 1, so once a value is at 1, it stays.

17
00:01:53,733 --> 00:02:03,066
A point with p(x) = x is a fixed point. Solving gives x cubed = x: the fixed points are −1, 0 and 1.

18
00:02:03,066 --> 00:02:08,766
So why does 0.1 walk away from 0 and toward 1? A picture makes it clear.

19
00:02:08,766 --> 00:02:13,633
Draw the curve y = p(x), and the diagonal y = x.

20
00:02:13,633 --> 00:02:19,233
To iterate on the picture, go from x up to the curve: that height is p(x).

21
00:02:19,233 --> 00:02:25,433
Then go across to the diagonal. That moves p(x) back onto the x axis as the new x.

22
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:30,533
Repeat. Starting at 0.3, the path climbs a staircase up to 1.

23
00:02:30,533 --> 00:02:35,400
From 1.2, it drops just below 1, then settles on 1 as well.

24
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:41,400
The fixed points −1, 0 and 1 are exactly where the curve meets the diagonal.

25
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:47,600
Why is 1 a magnet while 0 pushes things away? Look at the slope of p at each one.

26
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:55,900
At 0 the slope is 1.5, so a small offset grows by half each step. From 0.05 it drifts away.

27
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:08,233
At ±1 the slope is 0, so the error roughly squares each step. From 1.2: 0.2, then 0.064, then 0.006.

28
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:16,533
So 0 is unstable, like a hilltop, and ±1 are stable, like the bottoms of two valleys.

29
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:22,733
So far every start went to 1. But we don't get to choose σ. How large can it be?

30
00:03:22,733 --> 00:03:30,800
Try 1.5. p(1.5) = 0.56, and from there it climbs to 1. Still fine.

31
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:41,100
Now try 1.8. p(1.8) = −0.216. It turned negative, and then slides down to −1!

32
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:47,333
Where does the sign change? Factor p: p(x) is half of x times (3 minus x squared).

33
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:55,400
The second factor turns negative once x passes √3, about 1.73. And 1.8 is just past it.

34
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:02,800
Below √3 the curve stays positive, and it never rises above 1: its peak is p(1) = 1.

35
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:11,700
So one step lands between 0 and 1. There p(x) − x is half of x(1 − x squared), which is positive.

36
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:18,366
Each step goes up but never past 1. So every σ between 0 and √3 ends at +1.

37
00:04:18,366 --> 00:04:27,866
Exactly at √3, p(√3) = 0. One step lands on 0, the unstable fixed point, and stays forever.

38
00:04:27,866 --> 00:04:37,366
What about a much bigger start, like 3? p(3) = −9, and p(−9) = 351. It explodes.

39
00:04:37,366 --> 00:04:46,000
Past √3 the sign flips every step. But does the size shrink or grow? Compare |p(x)| to |x|.

40
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:56,200
For x past √3, the ratio is below 1 exactly when x squared is below 5, that is |x| < √5.

41
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:06,633
So √5, about 2.24, is the second key number. √3 decides flip or not; √5 decides shrink or grow.

42
00:05:06,633 --> 00:05:15,833
Right at the edge, p(√5) = −√5 and p(−√5) = √5. The cobweb becomes a square.

43
00:05:15,833 --> 00:05:23,933
So √5 neither settles nor explodes. It bounces between ±√5 forever: a period-2 orbit.

44
00:05:23,933 --> 00:05:35,866
Just past it, at 2.3, every step flips and grows: −2.63, 5.18, −61.8. It diverges.

45
00:05:35,866 --> 00:05:42,666
That leaves the gap between √3 and √5. There, each step flips the sign and shrinks the size.

46
00:05:42,666 --> 00:05:50,133
Shrinking, it must drop below √3 sooner or later. Then the flips stop, and it goes to ±1.

47
00:05:50,133 --> 00:05:59,500
1.8 went to −1. Is the whole gap like that? Let's test three close starts: 2.0, 2.2 and 2.23.

48
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:05,066
We plot only the size of each value, show its sign by color, and count the flips.

49
00:06:05,066 --> 00:06:10,266
Step by step: the sizes move left, and the colors alternate.

50
00:06:10,266 --> 00:06:18,000
2.0 flips once before dropping below √3. 2.2 flips twice, and 2.23 three times.

51
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,900
So 2.0 ends at −1, but 2.2 ends at +1, and 2.23 at −1 again!

52
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:32,333
From a positive start, an odd number of flips ends at −1, and an even number at +1.

53
00:06:32,333 --> 00:06:40,666
Tiny changes in the start, alternating answers. So where exactly does the count change?

54
00:06:40,666 --> 00:06:49,833
One flip or two? One step must land inside √3. The cutoff is where p(x) is exactly −√3. Call it b1.

55
00:06:49,833 --> 00:06:58,966
p is decreasing here, so every x between √3 and b1 lands between −√3 and 0: one flip, then −1.

56
00:06:58,966 --> 00:07:06,300
Both 1.8 and 2 are in this piece. In fact p(2) is exactly −1.

57
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:16,433
Keep going the same way: pick b2 with p(b2) = −b1, then b3 with p(b3) = −b2, and so on.

58
00:07:16,433 --> 00:07:28,133
Numerically b1 ≈ 2.1478, b2 ≈ 2.2212, b3 ≈ 2.2336. They creep up toward √5.

59
00:07:28,133 --> 00:07:34,633
Cut the gap at these points. Key fact: p maps each piece onto the mirror image of the one before.

60
00:07:34,633 --> 00:07:40,333
The first piece lands inside √3 on the negative side, so it goes to −1.

61
00:07:40,333 --> 00:07:47,766
p is odd, so mirror images have opposite fates. The second piece maps onto the mirror of the first: +1.

62
00:07:47,766 --> 00:07:53,833
The third maps onto the mirror of the second: −1 again. And so on, alternating.

63
00:07:53,833 --> 00:08:01,633
So a start in the n-th piece flips exactly n times: odd n ends at −1, even n at +1.

64
00:08:01,633 --> 00:08:08,633
The pieces pile up against √5. Zoom in, and the same pattern repeats again and again.

65
00:08:08,633 --> 00:08:15,033
Each piece is about a sixth of the one before, because the slope of p at √5 is −6.

66
00:08:15,033 --> 00:08:25,766
So between √3 and √5 there are infinitely many basins, alternating −1, +1, −1, +1, and so on.

67
00:08:25,766 --> 00:08:32,033
And the cut points themselves? b1 goes to −√3 in one step, and then to 0.

68
00:08:32,033 --> 00:08:41,666
b2 → −b1 → √3 → 0, and b3 → −b2 → b1 → −√3 → 0.

69
00:08:41,666 --> 00:08:49,866
Every cut point hits ±√3 exactly after a few steps, then sits on 0, the unstable fixed point.

70
00:08:49,866 --> 00:08:56,866
But they are single points: nudge one slightly, and it falls into a basin on either side.

71
00:08:56,866 --> 00:08:59,766
Here is the full answer to part (e).

72
00:08:59,766 --> 00:09:05,833
Below √3, σ goes to +1. Exactly at √3, it lands on 0.

73
00:09:05,833 --> 00:09:13,566
Between √3 and √5, −1 and +1 alternate with the number of flips; the cut points go to 0.

74
00:09:13,566 --> 00:09:19,766
At √5 it bounces forever, and beyond √5 it diverges.

75
00:09:19,766 --> 00:09:26,166
Back to the matrix. A real W can have singular values anywhere, some far above √5.

76
00:09:26,166 --> 00:09:32,033
This one at 2.9 would blow up. So before iterating, W must be scaled down.

77
00:09:32,033 --> 00:09:38,633
A common choice: divide by the Frobenius norm. It is never smaller than the largest singular value.

78
00:09:38,633 --> 00:09:44,766
After scaling, every singular value sits between 0 and 1, safely below √3.

79
00:09:44,766 --> 00:09:52,066
Now each one walks to +1. Small ones take longer, but all arrive, and W becomes U V transpose.

80
00:09:52,066 --> 00:09:58,400
The ellipse became a circle. And a matrix question turned into a puzzle about one number.

